<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/oahu-suburb.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/oahu-suburb-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/oahu-suburb.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/appraisal-specialties/">Specialties</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</span></nav><p class="tag">Appraisal specialty</p><h1>Homeowner Claim Appraisal for Hawaii Houses</h1><p class="lede">Hawaii homes are built differently, and repairing them costs differently. We help homeowners show what a covered loss truly takes to put right.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-light" href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>For</dt><dd>Owner-occupied homes</dd></div><div><dt>Covers</dt><dd>Dwelling &amp; other structures</dd></div><div><dt>Decides</dt><dd>Amount of loss</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why Hawaii homes are hard to estimate</h2>
<p>A carrier estimate built from generic pricing can miss a lot in Hawaii. Many older homes on Oahu, Kauai and the Big Island are single-wall construction, where the exterior boards are also the interior wall surface. Post-and-pier foundations, hip roofs with corrugated metal, termite-treated lumber and add-on ohana units are common. When the estimate treats your house like a tract home with drywall and a slab, the scope and the price can both fall short.</p>
<p>Then there is the cost of getting materials and people to the job. Nearly everything arrives by ocean freight, and neighbor-island jobs often add barge charges. After a large event, labor gets scarce. Hawaii General Excise Tax applies to most contractor work and is sometimes missing from estimates altogether.</p>
<h2>What we look at on a homeowner claim</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scope:</strong> every damaged component, including items hidden behind single-wall boards or under the floor system.</li>
<li><strong>Matching:</strong> whether repaired areas can reasonably match the rest of the roof, siding or flooring when profiles and finishes have been discontinued.</li>
<li><strong>Code items:</strong> what your county building department will require for the repair, and whether your policy includes ordinance or law coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Pricing:</strong> local labor, freight, disposal and GET, priced for your island rather than a mainland average.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation:</strong> how the carrier calculated actual cash value and what holdback you can recover once work is done.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Wear vs. sudden damage</b>Salt air corrosion, UV breakdown and termite damage are usually excluded, and carriers often point to them when a claim involves an older home. The panel decides the amount of covered damage; if the real dispute is whether the cause is covered at all, that is a coverage question for a Hawaii attorney.</div>
<h2>How the appraisal works for a homeowner</h2>
<p>Once appraisal is demanded, we inspect your home, build a line-item estimate and meet with the carrier’s appraiser. Most of the work is narrowing the difference: confirming measurements, agreeing on which items are damaged and resolving pricing. Items still in dispute go to the umpire. A signed award from two of the three panel members sets the amount.</p>
<h3>Useful tools before you call</h3>
<p>Our <a href="/island-claim-tools/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV, ACV and holdback calculator</a> shows how depreciation affects what you receive now versus later. The <a href="/island-claim-tools/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap and fee estimator</a> helps you judge whether the difference is large enough to make appraisal worthwhile. For background on corrosion disputes, read our guide to <a href="/hawaii-claim-guides/salt-air-corrosion-vs-storm-damage/">salt air corrosion versus storm damage</a>.</p>
<h3>Depreciation on island homes</h3>
<p>Carriers sometimes apply heavy depreciation to older homes, even to components that were recently replaced. Records of past roof, plumbing or electrical work help show their real age and condition.</p>
<h2>Homes we work on</h2>
<p>We see plantation cottages in Wahiawa and Haleiwa, hillside homes in Manoa and Kaimuki, newer subdivisions in Kapolei and Mililani, Upcountry homes in Kula, and off-grid Puna houses with rain catchment systems. Each has its own construction quirks and access challenges, and the estimate should reflect them.</p>
<p>Hawaii Insurance Appraiser is independent. We are not public adjusters, we do not negotiate the rest of your claim, and we do not promise results. What we offer is a careful, documented number and a clear presentation of it on the appraisal panel. If your homeowner claim has stalled over price, <a href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">reach out</a> with your policy and the carrier’s estimate.</p>
<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does a single-wall home cost more to repair?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>It can, because damage to the exterior boards is also damage to the interior surface, and replacement boards may need to be milled or sourced to match. A good estimate accounts for that.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal include my ohana unit?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>If the ohana unit is covered under your policy, usually as part of the dwelling or other structures, its damage can be included in the amount of loss the panel decides.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will appraisal help if my claim was denied for wear and tear?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Usually not on its own. A wear-and-tear denial is a coverage decision, and you should talk with a Hawaii attorney about it.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related specialties</h2><div class="related"><a href="/appraisal-specialties/condo-aoao-commercial-appraisal/"><small>Specialty</small><span>Condo, AOAO &amp; Commercial</span><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/appraisal-specialties/roof-hail-claim-appraisal/"><small>Specialty</small><span>Roof &amp; Hail Claims